CAT = "pltDiverse"; SEG = "DIV"
Q = [
("Language input that is slightly beyond an English learner's current level but understandable with support is called:",
 "Comprehensible input", ["Academic register", "Code switching", "Language transfer"],
 "Krashen argued that learners acquire language from input they can understand with the help of context, visuals and gestures."),
("Which support best helps an English learner in a second-grade science lesson on the water cycle?",
 "A labeled diagram and key words", ["A longer lecture at a slower pace", "A seat at the back to reduce stress", "English grammar lessons before science"],
 "Visuals, labeled diagrams and pre-taught key vocabulary make content comprehensible while the student builds English. Delaying content until English is mastered holds the student back."),
("Under IDEA, the written plan that sets a student's annual goals and the special education services the school will provide is the:",
 "Individualized Education Program", ["Section 504 accommodation plan", "Schoolwide behavior contract", "Response to Intervention log"],
 "IDEA requires an IEP for each eligible child, developed by a team that includes the parents. A 504 plan comes from a different law and lists accommodations."),
("Section 504, which protects students with disabilities in programs that receive federal funds, is part of which law?",
 "Rehabilitation Act of 1973", ["Every Student Succeeds Act", "FERPA", "Title IX of 1972"],
 "Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 bars disability discrimination in federally funded programs, including public schools."),
("IDEA's guarantee that each eligible child receives special education and related services at no cost to the family is called:",
 "Free appropriate public education", ["Least restrictive environment", "Child Find obligation", "Procedural due process"],
 "FAPE is the core IDEA entitlement: special education and related services, at public expense, designed to meet the child's needs through an IEP."),
("A teacher gives a student with a visual impairment a large-print copy of the same test the class takes. This is:",
 "An accommodation", ["A modification", "A curriculum waiver", "An alternate standard"],
 "An accommodation changes how a student accesses or shows learning without changing what is expected. A modification changes the content or standard itself."),
("A student with an IEP is assigned 10 spelling words instead of 20, chosen at a lower grade level. This is:",
 "A modification", ["An accommodation", "A related service", "An enrichment task"],
 "Reducing the number and the difficulty of the words changes what the student is expected to learn, which is a modification."),
("Which behavior is most typical of an intellectually gifted third grader?",
 "Asks questions beyond the lesson", ["Needs many repetitions to learn facts", "Avoids any open-ended task", "Prefers copying to creating work"],
 "Gifted learners often show intense curiosity, ask complex questions and learn new material quickly. Needing much repetition is not typical."),
("A teacher pre-tests a gifted student, skips the material she has mastered and uses the saved time for an independent project. This is:",
 "Curriculum compacting", ["Grade retention", "Ability tracking", "Peer tutoring"],
 "Curriculum compacting removes already-mastered content and replaces it with more challenging work."),
("A teacher notices a student squinting at the board, holding books very close and complaining of headaches. The best first step is to:",
 "Refer him for a vision check", ["Move him to the back row", "Mark him as off task in class", "Give him extra reading homework"],
 "These signs suggest a possible visual problem. The teacher should share observations through the school's referral process, such as the nurse."),
("Which classroom practice best responds to students' varied cultural backgrounds?",
 "Texts that reflect students' lives", ["Avoiding any mention of culture", "Grouping students by ethnicity", "Expecting one way of speaking"],
 "Culturally responsive teaching connects learning to students' experiences, including texts and examples that reflect who they are."),
("A student who is hungry and sleeps poorly because of an unstable home may fall behind. This shows that learning is affected by:",
 "Socioeconomic factors", ["Learning style alone", "Birth order in the family", "Handedness and grip"],
 "Poverty, housing instability and food insecurity affect attention, attendance and energy, so socioeconomic status is a variable teachers consider."),
("A first grader has trouble producing the r and s sounds clearly, and classmates sometimes cannot understand him. This area of exceptionality is:",
 "Speech or language", ["Auditory processing", "Emotional or behavioral", "Cognitive"],
 "Articulation difficulties fall under speech and language impairments, usually served by a speech-language pathologist."),
("IDEA requires schools to identify, locate and evaluate children who may have disabilities. This duty is called:",
 "Child Find", ["Least restrictive environment", "Due process hearing", "Stay put"],
 "Child Find obliges schools to seek out children who may need special education, including those not yet in school."),
("For a student with ADHD who loses focus during long assignments, which accommodation is most appropriate?",
 "Shorter tasks with checkpoints", ["Removing recess as a consequence", "Seating near the busy doorway", "One longer task to finish alone"],
 "Short, chunked tasks with checkpoints help a student sustain attention. Seating near a busy door adds distractions."),
("A parent who is deaf asks for a sign language interpreter at a parent-teacher conference. The school should:",
 "Provide an interpreter", ["Ask the child to interpret", "Hold the meeting by email only", "Decline since the child hears"],
 "The ADA requires public entities to communicate effectively with people with disabilities, including parents, which can mean providing an interpreter."),
("Which statement about learning differences between boys and girls is best supported?",
 "Variation within each is large", ["Girls cannot excel in math", "Boys learn only by moving around", "Gender fixes reading ability early"],
 "Differences within a gender are much larger than average differences between genders, so teachers should avoid gender stereotypes about ability."),
("A student's prior knowledge of farming helps her quickly understand a unit on plant growth. This shows that learning depends on:",
 "Prior experience", ["Chronological age alone", "Birth month", "The time of day"],
 "New learning builds on what students already know, so teachers assess and connect to prior knowledge."),
("A teacher suspects a student has a learning disability in reading. Before any change in placement, IDEA requires:",
 "Parent consent and an evaluation", ["A move to a resource room", "A note on the report card", "A decision by the teacher alone"],
 "IDEA requires informed parent consent and a full and individual evaluation before a child is found eligible and placed in special education."),
("An English learner answers questions about a story better when the teacher first lets small groups talk in their home language. This is best described as:",
 "Using the home language", ["Delaying English learning", "Lowering the content standards", "Separate classes by language"],
 "A student's first language supports understanding and transfer to English. Translanguaging talk builds comprehension rather than slowing English growth."),
]
